    "label": "10",
    "prompt": "Show that $x=-3+2\\sqrt{2}$ satisfies the equation $x^2+6x+1=0$",
    "layout": "list",
    "columns": null,
    "questionDiagrams": [],
    "children": [],
    "answer": {
      "short": "Substituting $x=-3+2\\sqrt{2}$ gives $x^2+6x+1=0$, so it satisfies the equation.",
      "worked": "For $x=-3+2\\sqrt{2}$,\n\n$$\\begin{align*}x^2+6x+1&=(-3+2\\sqrt{2})^2+6(-3+2\\sqrt{2})+1\\\\x^2+6x+1&=9-12\\sqrt{2}+8-18+12\\sqrt{2}+1\\\\x^2+6x+1&=0\\end{align*}$$\n\nTherefore, $x=-3+2\\sqrt{2}$ satisfies the equation.",
      "solutionDiagrams": []
    },
